@node What is a VW image?, , ,Images
@section What is a VW image?
@cindex image

All VW image classes store image data in the same way and provide the
same set of operations. An image is considered to be a two-dimensional
array of pixels stored in row-major form --- that is, stored along each
row first:
@example
    pixel0   pixel1   pixel2   pixel3
    pixel4   pixel5   pixel6   pixel7
    pixel8   pixel9   pixel10  pixel11
@end example
note that counting starts from the top-left of the image, not the
bottom-left as it would be on a graph.

For a monochrome image each pixel is simply an intensity value, but for
RGBA, YUVA or other formats, each pixel will contain several
elements. The image and pixel classes are templated on the type of data
stored so that you can produce a floating-point, double precision,
integer or pretty much any other numeric type image. For example, if
using @code{unsigned char} as the data type (which is a one-byte type)
then a mono pixel corresponds to a single byte in memory, whereas an
RGBA pixel will be four (R + G + B + alpha).



