@subsection Types of image

VW currently supports the following image types:
@table @code
@item ImageMono
a monochrome image with a single intensity value per pixel

@item ImageRGB
a colour image with red green and blue stored seperately. A fourth
value, for alpha, is also stored, ensuring that the pixels lie on 4-byte
word boundaries in memory.

@item ImageYUV
a colour image with intensity (Y) blue-yellow chromacity (U) and
red-green chromacity (V) stored separately. A fourth
value, for alpha, is also stored, ensuring that the pixels lie on 4-byte
word boundaries in memory.

@item ImageHSV
a colour image with hue (H) colour saturation (S) and
intensity value (V) stored separately. A fourth
value, for alpha, is also stored, ensuring that the pixels lie on 4-byte
word boundaries in memory. @strong{This image type is still
experimental, and should not be trusted with important data.}

@end table

Each of these image types has a corresponding pixel type for storing the
image data, ie. @code{PixelMono}, @code{PixelRGB}, @code{PixelYUV} and @code{PixelHSV}. 

@subsection Creating an image
@cindex image constructor
@findex GetWidth
@findex GetHeight
@findex IsAlloced

The VW images live in the VW namespace and can be created either with or
without allocating the size of the image:
@example
   VW::ImageRGB<unsigned char> my_rgb_image(320,240);
   VW::ImageMono<float> my_mono_image;
@end example
note the data type template specifications @code{<unsigned char>} and
@code{<float>} and that the RGB image is told to reserve memory for a width of 320 pixels and
height of 240. In the case of the mono image, the size can be specified
later using
@example
   my_mono_image.AllocImageData(320,240);
@end example
which reserves the right amount of memory for this image, or will be
determined automatically when loading an image.

You can test whether an image has had its data allocated using
@example
   if ( my_mono_image.IsAlloced() ) @{...@}
@end example
and once an image is allocated, the size can be recovered at any time
@example
   unsigned int w = my_mono_image.GetWidth();
   unsigned int h = my_mono_image.GetHeight();
@end example

@subsection Freeing an image
@findex FreeImageData

Once an image has been allocated (whether on construction or
afterwards), its data can be freed and the memory released at any time
using 
@example
   my_image.FreeImageData();
@end example
This allows the data to be
reallocated later at a different size.


@subsection Reading and writing
@cindex image reading
@cindex image loading
@cindex image writing
@cindex image saving
@cindex PNG
@cindex JPEG
@cindex GIF
@cindex TIFF
@cindex PGM
@cindex PPM
@cindex MIT
@cindex PS
@cindex EPS
@cindex EPSI
@findex ReadImage
@findex WriteImage

The image classes are only able to open the @code{PGM} (mono) and @code{PPM}
(colour) image formats natively. In order to load and save other formats
image input and output makes use of operating system functions. On Linux the
@uref{http://netpbm.sourceforge.net,NetPBM} graphics software is used, allowing
IO of @code{PGM, PPM, JPEG, PNG, GIF,
TIFF, MIT, PS, EPS} and @code{EPSI} formats, and on Windows the WinGDI interface is used, allowing use of all natively supported formats (these will vary according to which image format libraries are registered with the system).

For an image that has its memory allocated, the size of the stored image
must match the image dimensions. If the size hasn't yet been allocated,
the image will take the size of the stored image. Loading any image
format takes the form
@example
   my_image.ReadImage("some_image.jpeg");
@end example
The image type is determined from the filename extension (how very
Microsoft).

Storing of an image is similarly simple
@example
   my_image.WriteImage("another_image.png");
@end example

Both loading and saving involve some conversions unless PPMs/PGMs are used. If you need fast IO, and CPU usage is
more important than disk bandwidth these native formats should be used.


