@node Manipulating the image, , ,Images
@section Manipulating the image

There are a number of ways to access the image data according to how
important speed is.

@subsection The easy way
@findex GetPixel
@findex SetPixel
@findex ScaleIntensity

The first (and most obvious) method to get/set a pixel is to use the
@code{GetPixel(x,y)} and @code{SetPixel(x,y,p)} functions. For example, to scan an
entire RGB image halving the intensities
@example
   for (unsigned int y = 0; y < my_rgb_image.GetHeight(); y++) @{
      for (unsigned int x = 0; x < my_rgb_image.GetWidth(); x++) @{

         VW::PixelRGB<unsigned char> p = my_rgb_image.GetPixel(x,y);
         p.ScaleIntensitiy(0.5);
         my_rgb_image.SetPixel(x,y,p);

      @}
   @}
@end example

To actually access the raw data, the pixel classes provide public access
to thier data, whether its @code{r}, @code{g}, @code{b}, @code{alpha}
(RGB),  @code{y}, @code{u}, @code{v}, @code{alpha} (YUV) or @code{y}
(Mono).

@subsection The fast way

The second approach uses pointers to the start of each row, and pointer
arithmetic to navigate along the row. Each image
class provides three type definitions:
@table @code
@item DataType 
The type of the pixel elements (eg @code{unsigned char})

@item PixelType 
The type of the pixel class (eg @code{PixelRGB<unsigned char>})

@item PixelPtr
The type of a pointer to the pixel class (eg @code{PixelRGB<unsigned char>*})

@end table
and @code{my_image.GetRowStart(y)} returns a @code{PixelPtr} to the
start of row @code{y}. The following code is functionally identical to
the above example, but usually faster
@example
   for (unsigned int y = 0; y < my_rgb_image.GetHeight(); y++) @{
      typename VW::ImageRGB<unsigned char>::PixelPtr p = my_rgb_image.GetRowStart(y);

      for (unsigned int x = 0; x < my_rgb_image.GetWidth(); x++,p++) @{

         p->ScaleIntensitiy(0.5);

      @}
   @}
@end example
Note that in this case @code{p} points to the pixel in the image, not a
copy of it as previously.

@subsection Playing with colours

Finally in this section, here is an example that manipulates the
underlying colour data in each pixel --- in this case swapping red and
blue for that psychadelic feel:
@example
   for (unsigned int y = 0; y < my_rgb_image.GetHeight(); y++) @{
      typename VW::ImageRGB<unsigned char>::PixelPtr p = my_rgb_image.GetRowStart(y);

      for (unsigned int x = 0; x < my_rgb_image.GetWidth(); x++,p++) @{

         unsigned char t = p->r;
         p->r = p->b;
         p->b = t;

      @}
   @}
@end example



