@node Converting between image types, , ,Images
@section Converting between image types
@findex ImageCopy

The @code{VW::ImageCopy} functions provide methods for copying and
converting images. The function is templated on the two image types and
requires two equal-sized images (or an output image that has not yet had
its data allocated). For example
@multitable  @columnfractions .5 .5
@item @code{VW::ImageCopy(my_mono_image, my_rgb_image);}
@tab copies a monochrome image into an RGB image, setting each of RGB to
the mono value.

@item @code{VW::ImageCopy(my_rgb_image, my_yuv_image);}
@tab converts an RGB image into a YUV image.

@item @code{VW::ImageCopy(mono_uchar_image, mono_float_image);}
@tab converts an unsigned char type mono image into a floating point one.

@end multitable

Note that it is possible to convert @emph{either} the pixel type (YUV,RGB etc) @emph{or} the data type (unsigned char, float etc), but not both at once.

@subsection A note about YUV

Note that the balance of colour information in YUV is quite different
from RGB, since blue
is under-represented and green is over-represented when compared to the RGB
space. For example, using the standard conversion to YUV the red
information remains at about 100%, the green 140% and blue 60% (which is why chroma-keying on
video is always done against green backgrounds, whereas for films it is
done against blue). Together with rounding error, this means that
converting from RGB to YUV and back again invariably results in fairly
poor colour representation in the image, with blues particularly vulnerable.

